    {
      "id": "ad46156c-9c68-abaa-399e-6f9a38650739",
      "object": "block",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": {
        "rich_text": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": {
              "content": "Regulatory considerations:\n\nGLBA (Gramm-Leach-Bliley): Financial institutions must protect consumer financial data. Sharing payment histories with sales may violate reuse limitations. Vaulta isn't technically a bank but we operate under similar expectations given our financial data handling.\n\nCCPA/CPRA: California users can request to know exactly how their data is used. Our privacy policy says nothing about internal sales team access to payment data. Retroactive policy updates are legally questionable.\n\nCFPB: Consumer Financial Protection Bureau has been going after fintech companies hard this year. A single user complaint about this feature could trigger an investigation.\n\nPCI DSS: Not directly applicable since we aren't storing card numbers, but the spirit of PCI is to limit payment data access. This goes the opposite direction.\n\nMA Attorney General: Has been active on consumer data protection. We are headquartered in MA.\n\nReputational: Venmo got destroyed for public transaction data. Cash App in the news constantly for data handling. We don't need to be next.",
              "link": null
            },
            "plain_text": "Regulatory considerations:\n\nGLBA (Gramm-Leach-Bliley): Financial institutions must protect consumer financial data. Sharing payment histories with sales may violate reuse limitations. Vaulta isn't technically a bank but we operate under similar expectations given our financial data handling.\n\nCCPA/CPRA: California users can request to know exactly how their data is used. Our privacy policy says nothing about internal sales team access to payment data. Retroactive policy updates are legally questionable.\n\nCFPB: Consumer Financial Protection Bureau has been going after fintech companies hard this year. A single user complaint about this feature could trigger an investigation.\n\nPCI DSS: Not directly applicable since we aren't storing card numbers, but the spirit of PCI is to limit payment data access. This goes the opposite direction.\n\nMA Attorney General: Has been active on consumer data protection. We are headquartered in MA.\n\nReputational: Venmo got destroyed for public transaction data. Cash App in the news constantly for data handling. We don't need to be next.",
            "href": null,
            "annotations": {
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false,
              "strikethrough": false,
              "underline": false,
              "code": false,
              "color": "default"
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      "parent": {
        "type": "page_id",
        "page_id": "1f19a0f0-0357-944a-c317-d0a99a821e14"
      },
      "created_time": "2026-01-12T16:00:00.000Z",
      "created_by_id": "ca6a6e7a-9cc9-94b0-d147-cf4df0202707",
      "last_edited_time": "2026-01-12T17:00:00.000Z",
      "last_edited_by_id": "ca6a6e7a-9cc9-94b0-d147-cf4df0202707",
