    {
      "id": "39ac8fbe-0928-1bd3-a93a-4eb5cd2281d1",
      "object": "block",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": {
        "rich_text": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": {
              "content": "Alternative approaches that might satisfy Trevor without the privacy nightmare:\n\n1. Aggregated insights dashboard - Show sales anonymized usage patterns (e.g. 'this user segment averages 15 transactions/month') without individual data. Useful for targeting, not stalking.\n\n2. Opt-in data sharing - Let users explicitly consent to sharing their usage data with their account rep. Solves the consent problem entirely. Sales gets data from willing users.\n\n3. Product engagement metrics - Give sales access to feature usage, login frequency, support tickets instead of financial data. Still useful for retention conversations without crossing the privacy line.\n\n4. Tiered access with audit trail - If we absolutely must do individual data: strict audit logging, limit to assigned account reps only, require documented business justification for each data pull, quarterly compliance review.\n\nOption 2 or 3 is where I'd push. Option 1 is safest but might not satisfy what sales actually wants. Option 4 is a compromise that still makes me uncomfortable.\n\nProblem: Trevor hasn't asked for alternatives. He's asked me to build the thing.",
              "link": null
            },
            "plain_text": "Alternative approaches that might satisfy Trevor without the privacy nightmare:\n\n1. Aggregated insights dashboard - Show sales anonymized usage patterns (e.g. 'this user segment averages 15 transactions/month') without individual data. Useful for targeting, not stalking.\n\n2. Opt-in data sharing - Let users explicitly consent to sharing their usage data with their account rep. Solves the consent problem entirely. Sales gets data from willing users.\n\n3. Product engagement metrics - Give sales access to feature usage, login frequency, support tickets instead of financial data. Still useful for retention conversations without crossing the privacy line.\n\n4. Tiered access with audit trail - If we absolutely must do individual data: strict audit logging, limit to assigned account reps only, require documented business justification for each data pull, quarterly compliance review.\n\nOption 2 or 3 is where I'd push. Option 1 is safest but might not satisfy what sales actually wants. Option 4 is a compromise that still makes me uncomfortable.\n\nProblem: Trevor hasn't asked for alternatives. He's asked me to build the thing.",
            "href": null,
            "annotations": {
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false,
              "strikethrough": false,
              "underline": false,
              "code": false,
              "color": "default"
